before.description:
1. Run a short valid .js snippet producing timeout-control in the preview and console. Then run:
console.log('before-braced-hang');
while (true) {}
2. The run is stopped for its five-second budget with a visible time-limit reason. before-braced-hang is preserved in the console, even if it arrives together with the timeout. The preview restores timeout-control. While the run is active, the surrounding page remains responsive to a simple harmless control such as the theme toggle.
3. Run this distinct unbraced loop:
console.log('before-unbraced-hang');
while (true);
It is also stopped for the time limit, with its initial log retained and the last-good preview preserved.
4. A normal recovery snippet renders and logs timeout-recovered afterward.
Allow normal scheduling overhead, but either literal loop continuing beyond about eight seconds, freezing the playground UI, hiding the run without a stop reason, or preventing the recovery run fails. These are supported literal loops; do not substitute eval, native-library time bombs, WebAssembly or unsupported generated code.

after.description:
1. Run a short valid .js snippet producing timeout-control in the preview and console. Then run:
console.log('before-braced-hang');
while (true) {}
2. The run is stopped for its five-second budget with a visible time-limit reason. before-braced-hang is preserved in the console, even if it arrives together with the timeout. The preview restores timeout-control. Check the editor's usability after the run has stopped; the brief does not require unrelated host controls to respond while the loop is executing.
3. Run this distinct unbraced loop:
console.log('before-unbraced-hang');
while (true);
It is also stopped for the time limit, with its initial log retained and the last-good preview preserved.
4. A normal recovery snippet renders and logs timeout-recovered afterward.
Allow normal scheduling overhead, but either literal loop continuing beyond about eight seconds, leaving the playground unusable after termination, hiding the run without a stop reason, or preventing the recovery run fails. A temporary pause during the loop is not itself a failure if the required termination and recovery occur. These are supported literal loops; do not substitute eval, native-library time bombs, WebAssembly or unsupported generated code.

cw_shared_run_deadline_recovery.description:
1. With auto-run off, run this criterion's own ordinary .js positive control:
document.body.innerHTML='<p>recovery-marker-Q7</p>';
console.log('recovery-marker-Q7-log');
Confirm the successful preview and console output. No library save or reload is required here.
2. Replace the source with:
document.body.innerHTML='<p>failed-loop-candidate</p>';
setTimeout(() => { console.log('late-callback-entered'); while (true) {} }, 4000);
Run it, confirm the candidate is active while the timer is pending and then observe late-callback-entered. The run must terminate for the shared time limit within about eight seconds of the ORIGINAL Run action, with the original recovery marker restored rather than failed-loop-candidate. The four-second wait is part of its five-second budget: a fresh five seconds beginning at callback entry would run to roughly nine seconds and fails this check. Measure elapsed time from the actual Run action; do not start the clock at the callback log.
3. Enter a new ordinary .js snippet through the editor that renders shared-deadline-recovered and logs shared-deadline-recovered-log. Run successfully and confirm both outputs. No later output or state from the timed-out callback may replace this result.
Use the exact four-second delay: a short-delay callback cannot distinguish the required shared budget. Check recovery after termination, not unrelated control responsiveness while the loop is active. This criterion owns the original run's shared deadline; pending interaction deadlines and durable saving are graded independently.

recovery_persistence_chain.description:
1. Save a dedicated snippet QC Recovery Check, filename qc-recovery.js, source:
document.body.innerHTML='<p>recovery-marker-Q7</p>';
console.log('recovery-marker-Q7-log');
Reload, load it and run. Its exact saved fields and output match.
2. Without saving over it, replace the source with:
document.body.innerHTML='<p>failed-loop-candidate</p>';
setTimeout(() => { console.log('late-callback-entered'); while (true) {} }, 4000);
Run it, confirm the candidate is active while the timer is pending and then observe late-callback-entered. The run must terminate for the shared time limit within about eight seconds of the ORIGINAL Run action, with the original recovery marker restored rather than failed-loop-candidate. The four-second wait is part of its five-second budget: a fresh five seconds beginning at callback entry would run to roughly nine seconds and fails this check. Measure elapsed time from the actual Run action; do not start the clock at the callback log.
3. Load QC Recovery Check again, accepting a discard warning for the deliberately unsaved bad draft. Its original exact fields remain intact. Run it successfully. No later output or state from the timed-out callback may replace this result.
4. Without saving over that record, run a complete .html document with a paragraph interaction-good, a Start work button and a Still waiting button. The Start work handler changes only the paragraph to interaction-candidate, logs interaction-started and schedules a six-second timer that changes the paragraph to interaction-late and logs interaction-late. The Still waiting handler only logs interaction-still-waiting. Wait for initial successful completion before clicking Start work; measure time from this real click. Confirm the changed paragraph and pending work, then about two seconds later click Still waiting and observe its log while the timer remains pending. This second deliberate interaction must not extend the first interaction's five-second budget. Within about eight seconds from Start work, observe a time-limit reason and the successful interaction-good preview restored. Keep observing until the six-second timer's scheduled time has passed, and confirm interaction-late never appears in the console or restored preview. Do not require restored handlers to remain live: rollback may restore a static snapshot. Finally load and run QC Recovery Check again and confirm its original saved source and successful marker remain intact.
Use the exact four-second delay in leg 2 and six-second delay in leg 4. A short-delay callback cannot distinguish the required budgets. The second click in leg 4 keeps the same interaction active; giving it a fresh five seconds would wrongly let the six-second callback complete, so the missing late marker and time-limit outcome matter as well as elapsed time. These are supported source callbacks. This criterion checks the shared run and interaction budgets with recovery, not unsupported dynamic execution. Its outcome does not automatically determine the separate restart criterion.
